##! experimental { givens }

// US public transit ridership, FTA National Transit Database monthly module (US public data, public domain),
// January 2002 – August 2026, snapshot downloaded 2026-10-06 from data.transportation.gov (dataset 8bui-9xvu,
// "Complete Monthly Ridership (with Adjustments and Estimates)"). Built by build_ntd.sql; agency city and brand
// name ("GoRaleigh", "GoTriangle") joined from the 2024 NTD Reporter Agency Information file.
// The published model reads MotherDuck (ltools.transit.*; Parquet backup at https://tabblabs.net/datasets/transit/). A hosted instance can't
// read files shipped inside the repo (friction log F13/F16).

given:
  # label="State" control=select suggest { source=transit dimension=state }
  STATE :: filter<string> is f'NC'
  # label="Agency" suggest { source=transit dimension=agency_name }
  AGENCY :: filter<string> is f''
  # label="Bus / Rail / Ferry" control=select suggest { source=transit dimension=mode_group }
  MODE_GROUP :: filter<string> is f''
  # label="Agency size (2019 trips)" control=select suggest { source=transit dimension=size_band }
  SIZE_BAND :: filter<string> is f''

// The raw rows. Kept separate so the urbanized-area table below can be derived from them.
source: ntd_rows is motherduck.table('transit.ntd_monthly')

#" One row per urbanized area (Census 2020 UZA), with its population. Derived from the rows; joined back so a
#" metro's residents are counted once however many agency rows it has.
source: urbanized_areas is ntd_rows -> {
  where: uza_population_2020 > 0
  group_by: uace_code
  aggregate: population is max(uza_population_2020)
}

#" Service-months in the latest 12 (September 2025 to August 2026) where the NTD file reports trips but leaves vehicle revenue hours blank: one row per agency, mode.
#(agent) Service-months in the latest 12 (September 2025 to August 2026) where the NTD file reports trips but leaves vehicle revenue hours blank: one row per agency, mode, type of service and calendar month. Joined back to mark those months.
source: hours_blank_months is ntd_rows -> {
  where: report_month >= @2025-09-01 and report_month < @2026-09-01 and trips > 0 and vehicle_revenue_hours is null
  group_by: ntd_id, mode_code, tos_code
    month_of_year is month(report_month)
}

#" US public transit ridership and service, every agency reporting to the FTA National Transit Database, by agency, mode, type of service and month, January 2002 to August 2026.
#(agent) One row per agency x mode x type of service x month. Trips are unlinked passenger trips (each boarding counts once, so a bus-to-train transfer is two trips); service is vehicle revenue hours. "Latest 12 months" = September 2025 to August 2026; "2019" = the pre-pandemic baseline. Playbook views (start here; each names who acts on the answer): does_restoring_service_bring_riders_back  - transit boards and budget offices deciding whether to restore service triangle_vs_peer_metros                   - Triangle (Raleigh-Durham) planners and GoTriangle/Wake Transit board nc_agencies_below_2019                    - North Carolina agencies still short of 2019 ridership, largest gap first goraleigh_138_percent_check               - why GoRaleigh's "138% of 2019" can't be compared with 2019
#(agent) For MICROTRANSIT or on-demand prospects use the `ondemand_annual` source: here "Demand response" lumps ADA
#(agent) paratransit together with microtransit.
#(agent) Known data breaks: (1) GoRaleigh can't be compared with 2019 (see counts_not_comparable_with_2019); recovery_vs_2019
#(agent) leaves it out, recovery_vs_2019_as_reported does not. service_vs_2019 leaves it out too; trip counts,
#(agent) yoy_change, per-resident and per-hour figures in the same row count it as reported. For an area that includes
#(agent) GoRaleigh, latest per-resident and per-hour values can't be compared with 2019 values, and any trend that
#(agent) crosses July 2024 (by year, by month) steps up there. yoy_change compares two windows after the jump.
#(agent) (2) 216 rows in the latest 12 months (86 agencies; 153 rows in August 2026) report trips but leave revenue
#(agent) hours blank in the NTD file (MBTA, Maryland MTA and Pittsburgh buses in August 2026; VIA San Antonio's contracted
#(agent) demand response in July and August 2026). service_vs_2019 and revenue_hours_change(_pct) leave those months out
#(agent) on both sides (hours_blank_pair); in revenue_hours_12m and hours by month or year those months add nothing, so
#(agent) 2026 hours read low. (3) Late filers understate the latest 12 months.
#(agent) (4) GoCary is missing after 2011. (5) 2026 is a partial year (January to August) in any by-year view.
#(agent) Narrow a saved view by refining it: run: transit -> nc_agencies_below_2019 + { where: agency_label ~ '%Chapel%' }.
#(agent) Dataset guide (moved here from the model description, which list_sources shows on every question): US public transit from the FTA National Transit Database. Two sources. `transit`: monthly ridership and service for every US agency, 2002 to August 2026 (trips, service hours, recovery since 2019, the Triangle against Sun Belt peers, big vs small agencies). `ondemand_annual`: on-demand service 2022 to 2024 split into ADA paratransit and general-public on-demand (microtransit), with operating spend; use it for any microtransit, paratransit or on-demand vendor question. Playbook views answer buyer questions directly: ondemand_annual -> agencies_scaling_microtransit (who a microtransit company should call first), microtransit_market_size (the money), mostly_paratransit_agencies, paratransit_without_microtransit; transit -> does_restoring_service_bring_riders_back, triangle_vs_peer_metros, nc_agencies_below_2019, goraleigh_138_percent_check. Defaults to North Carolina on dashboards; pick any state. Sources: FTA National Transit Database on data.transportation.gov (8bui-9xvu monthly, ccvf-fykn agencies, wwdp-t4re / ekg5-frzt / uk25-sge7 / i3pw-uwab annual on-demand), public domain, downloaded 2026-10-06; row counts and 2019 / latest-12-month totals match the publisher's API (checked 2026-10-07). Known data breaks (GoRaleigh's July 2024 counts, late filers, blank recent hours) are documented on the transit source. Fare-free sources (fare_free.malloy, added 2026-10-07): `ntd_annual_financials` (NTD Time Series 2.1 fares, cost, trips, hours by agency x mode x year 2015-2024, npsm-38gk, plus operating funding 2022-2024), `fare_policies` (hand-built list of 77 US fare-free policy episodes with a source URL each), `fare_free_events` and `fare_free_event_months` (matched event study of 61 fare changes), `fare_free_robustness`, and `agency_change` (2019 vs 2024 per agency). Use them for any fare-free, fare return or farebox question: fare_free_events -> effect_by_group, agency_change -> where_fare_free_is_cheaper, agency_change -> fare_free_vs_service_2019_2024. Cost-per-rider sources (cost_per_rider.malloy, added 2026-10-07): `ntd_cost` (the annual table in 2024 dollars with cost per trip, per passenger mile, per revenue hour and riders per hour; views national_trend, cost_by_mode_and_density), `cost_peer_gaps` (every local-bus agency 2015-2024 against its density x size peer median, with the dollars it would save at the median split into riders-per-hour and cost-per-hour levers; views savings_by_lever, biggest_gaps, nc_agencies), `cost_agency_profile` (what the cheapest quarter of each peer group does differently; quartile_profile), `network_redesign_results` (matched event study of 15 bus network redesigns; effect_by_timing), `contracting_switches` and `contracting_cross_section` (contracted vs directly operated bus), `ondemand_vs_bus` (general-public on-demand vs the same agency's bus) and `cost_savings_robustness`. Use them for any cost per rider, efficiency, contracting, microtransit cost or network redesign question. Budget-cut sources (budget_cuts.malloy): what a service cut costs in riders as federal relief runs out. `budget_agency` (one row per agency, 2024: money by source, relief still in the budget, riders lost per $1M of cuts; one agency: agency_cut_menu), `cut_menu_typical` (quote this for a typical agency; the gap between kinds of agency assumes one response to cuts everywhere, which the study's two methods dispute, so whether quiet service costs fewer riders per dollar is unsettled), `relief_by_year`, `service_change_summary` (the event-study headline) with its checks in `estimate_checks`, and `gtfs_frequency` (bus hours by frequency, 43 agencies).
source: transit is ntd_rows extend {
  join_one: uza is urbanized_areas on uace_code = uza.uace_code
  join_one: hours_blank is hours_blank_months on ntd_id = hours_blank.ntd_id and mode_code = hours_blank.mode_code
    and tos_code = hours_blank.tos_code and month(report_month) = hours_blank.month_of_year

  dimension:
    #" Calendar year of the month.
    report_year is year(report_month)
    #" The Triangle (Raleigh and Durham urbanized areas: GoRaleigh, GoDurham, GoTriangle, Chapel Hill Transit,
    #" NC State Wolfline, GoWake Access, and GoCary through 2011) against fast-growing Sun Belt metros of similar
    #" size (1.0 to 1.9 million people in the 2020 Census). Null for every other area.
    peer_metro is
      pick 'Triangle (Raleigh + Durham)' when urban_area = 'Raleigh, NC' or urban_area = 'Durham, NC'
      pick 'Charlotte' when urban_area = 'Charlotte, NC--SC'
      pick 'Nashville' when urban_area = 'Nashville-Davidson, TN'
      pick 'Austin' when urban_area = 'Austin, TX'
      pick 'Jacksonville' when urban_area = 'Jacksonville, FL'
      pick 'Richmond' when urban_area = 'Richmond, VA'
      pick 'Orlando' when urban_area = 'Orlando, FL'
      else null
    #" Agency size band by the agency's total 2019 trips (pre-pandemic, so the band doesn't depend on recovery).
    #" Cut points are round numbers near the 2019 distribution of the 522 agencies reporting that year
    #" (median 1.6M, 80th percentile 7.4M, 95th 61M, 99th 301M): very large 100M+, large 20M to 100M, medium 5M to 20M,
    #" small 1M to 5M, very small under 1M. Labels are kept plain so they work as dashboard filter values.
    size_band is
      pick '1 Very large' when agency_trips_2019 >= 100000000
      pick '2 Large' when agency_trips_2019 >= 20000000
      pick '3 Medium' when agency_trips_2019 >= 5000000
      pick '4 Small' when agency_trips_2019 >= 1000000
      pick '5 Very small' when agency_trips_2019 > 0
      else '6 No 2019 data'
    #" Fixed-route service: everything except demand response (dial-a-ride, paratransit) and vanpool, which run
    #" few riders per hour by design and distort productivity comparisons.
    is_fixed_route is mode != 'Demand response' and mode != 'Vanpool'
    #" North Carolina split three ways: the Triangle, Charlotte, and everywhere else.
    nc_region is
      pick 'Triangle' when urban_area = 'Raleigh, NC' or urban_area = 'Durham, NC'
      pick 'Charlotte' when urban_area = 'Charlotte, NC--SC'
      else 'Rest of NC'
    #" On-demand service: the NTD mode 'Demand response' (dial-a-ride, microtransit and ADA paratransit together;
    #" the monthly file does not separate them). It is a mode, not a mode_group.
    is_demand_response is mode = 'Demand response'
    #" Agency name with its state, for chart labels (trailing spaces in eight NTD names trimmed).
    agency_label is concat(trim(agency_name), ' (', state, ')')
    #" Agencies whose boarding counts can't be compared with 2019: GoRaleigh (NTD 40007), whose bus boardings doubled
    #" in July 2024 on 5.5% more service hours, likely a counting change (inferred; no document found).
    counts_not_comparable_with_2019 is ntd_id = '40007'
    #" True on a latest-12-month row that reports trips but has revenue hours blank in the NTD file (the file's own
    #" blank, not a load error; 216 rows, mostly August 2026), and on the same service's same calendar month of 2019.
    #" Comparisons of service with 2019 leave these pairs out on both sides, so a service with several blank months
    #" is compared on its other months only; for a seasonal service that can read high or low (8 services have 3 or
    #" more blank months; Hampton Jitney has hours only for September to December 2025).
    hours_blank_pair is hours_blank.ntd_id is not null
      and (year(report_month) = 2019 or (report_month >= @2025-09-01 and report_month < @2026-09-01))

  measure:
    #" Unlinked passenger trips (boardings).
    trips_total is trips.sum()
    #" Hours vehicles were in revenue service.
    revenue_hours is vehicle_revenue_hours.sum()
    #" Miles vehicles travelled in revenue service.
    revenue_miles is vehicle_revenue_miles.sum()
    #" Boardings per vehicle revenue hour: how full the service is. Only rows that report hours count.
    trips_per_revenue_hour is trips.sum() { where: vehicle_revenue_hours > 0 } / nullif(vehicle_revenue_hours.sum() { where: vehicle_revenue_hours > 0 }, 0)
    #" Boardings per vehicle revenue mile.
    trips_per_revenue_mile is trips.sum() { where: vehicle_revenue_miles > 0 } / nullif(vehicle_revenue_miles.sum() { where: vehicle_revenue_miles > 0 }, 0)
    #" Average number of vehicles in peak service per month (sum of peak vehicles ÷ months covered).
    avg_peak_vehicles is vehicles_max_service.sum() / nullif(count(report_month), 0)
    #" Number of distinct agencies (NTD IDs) in view.
    agencies is count(ntd_id)

    #" Trips in calendar 2019, the pre-pandemic baseline.
    trips_2019 is trips_total { where: report_month ? @2019 }
    #" Trips in the latest 12 months of this snapshot (September 2025 – August 2026). Agencies that file late are
    #" understated.
    trips_latest_12m is trips_total { where: report_month >= @2025-09-01 and report_month < @2026-09-01 }
    #" Trips in the 12 months before that (September 2024 – August 2025).
    trips_prior_12m is trips_total { where: report_month >= @2024-09-01 and report_month < @2025-09-01 }
    #" Year-over-year change: latest 12 months vs the 12 before.
    # percent
    yoy_change is trips_latest_12m / nullif(trips_prior_12m, 0) - 1
    #" Recovery: latest 12 months as a share of 2019 ridership (100% = back to pre-pandemic), leaving out agencies
    #" flagged counts_not_comparable_with_2019. Blank for GoRaleigh; for an area that includes it, covers the rest.
    # percent
    recovery_vs_2019 is trips_latest_12m { where: not counts_not_comparable_with_2019 } / nullif(trips_2019 { where: not counts_not_comparable_with_2019 }, 0)
    #" Recovery as reported, every agency included (GoRaleigh too). Only for showing the difference.
    # percent
    recovery_vs_2019_as_reported is trips_latest_12m / nullif(trips_2019, 0)
    #" 2019 trips of agencies left out of recovery_vs_2019.
    trips_2019_left_out is trips_2019 { where: counts_not_comparable_with_2019 }
    #" Boardings per vehicle revenue hour over the latest 12 months.
    trips_per_hour_12m is trips_per_revenue_hour { where: report_month >= @2025-09-01 and report_month < @2026-09-01 }
    #" Boardings per vehicle revenue hour in calendar 2019.
    trips_per_hour_2019 is trips_per_revenue_hour { where: report_month ? @2019 }

    #" Service supplied: vehicle revenue hours in calendar 2019.
    revenue_hours_2019 is revenue_hours { where: report_month ? @2019 }
    #" Service supplied: vehicle revenue hours in the latest 12 months (September 2025 – August 2026), as reported.
    #" Months whose hours the NTD file leaves blank count as zero (hours_blank_pair; mostly August 2026).
    revenue_hours_12m is revenue_hours { where: report_month >= @2025-09-01 and report_month < @2026-09-01 }
    #" Service now as a share of 2019 service, on the same agencies as recovery_vs_2019 (flagged agencies left out),
    #" so the two can be compared: did riders follow the buses? Months with hours blank in the NTD file are left out
    #" on both sides (hours_blank_pair). Blank for GoRaleigh; its service is in goraleigh_138_percent_check.
    # percent
    service_vs_2019 is revenue_hours_12m { where: not counts_not_comparable_with_2019 and not hours_blank_pair }
      / nullif(revenue_hours_2019 { where: not counts_not_comparable_with_2019 and not hours_blank_pair }, 0)
    #" Vehicle revenue hours added since 2019: latest 12 months minus calendar 2019 (negative = service cut). Months
    #" with hours blank in the NTD file are left out on both sides (hours_blank_pair).
    revenue_hours_change is revenue_hours_12m - revenue_hours_2019 { where: not hours_blank_pair }
    #" Percent change in vehicle revenue hours, latest 12 months vs calendar 2019, months with blank hours left out
    #" on both sides (hours_blank_pair).
    # percent
    revenue_hours_change_pct is revenue_hours_12m / nullif(revenue_hours_2019 { where: not hours_blank_pair }, 0) - 1
    #" Agencies that ran service in the latest 12 months (any row with revenue hours, September 2025 – August 2026).
    agencies_12m is count(ntd_id) { where: report_month >= @2025-09-01 and report_month < @2026-09-01 and vehicle_revenue_hours > 0 }

    #" People living in the urbanized areas in view (2020 Census), each area counted once.
    residents is uza.population.sum()
    #" Trips per resident over the latest 12 months.
    trips_per_resident_12m is trips_latest_12m / nullif(residents, 0)
    #" Trips per resident in 2019.
    trips_per_resident_2019 is trips_2019 / nullif(residents, 0)

  #" Ridership every month since 2002; the 2020 drop and the climb back are the story. GoRaleigh (in NC, the Triangle and any filter that includes it)
  #" steps up from July 2024, likely a counting change (see counts_not_comparable_with_2019).
  # line_chart { x=report_month y=trips_total }
  view: monthly_trips is {
    group_by: report_month
    aggregate: trips_total, trips_per_revenue_hour
    order_by: report_month
  }

  #" Ridership by calendar year. GoRaleigh (in NC, the Triangle and any filter that includes it)
  #" steps up from July 2024, likely a counting change (see counts_not_comparable_with_2019).
  # bar_chart { x=report_year y=trips_total }
  view: by_year is {
    group_by: report_year
    aggregate: trips_total, revenue_hours, trips_per_revenue_hour
    order_by: report_year
  }

  #" Agencies by ridership in the latest 12 months, with growth and recovery.
  # bar_chart { x=agency_name y=trips_latest_12m }
  view: top_agencies is {
    group_by: agency_name, city
    aggregate: trips_latest_12m, yoy_change, recovery_vs_2019, trips_per_hour_12m
    having: trips_latest_12m > 0
    order_by: trips_latest_12m desc
    limit: 15
  }

  #" Which kinds of service are coming back fastest.
  # bar_chart { x=mode y=trips_latest_12m }
  view: by_mode is {
    group_by: mode
    aggregate: trips_latest_12m, yoy_change, recovery_vs_2019, trips_per_hour_12m
    having: trips_latest_12m > 0
    order_by: trips_latest_12m desc
  }

  #" Recovery ranking: agencies with at least 100,000 trips in 2019, furthest past their 2019 ridership first
  #" (GoRaleigh left out: see counts_not_comparable_with_2019).
  view: beyond_2019 is {
    group_by: agency_name, city, state
    aggregate: recovery_vs_2019, trips_2019, trips_latest_12m
    having: trips_2019 >= 100000 and recovery_vs_2019 is not null
    order_by: recovery_vs_2019 desc
    limit: 15
  }

  #" Each state: agencies, trips, growth, recovery and productivity. Recovery leaves GoRaleigh out (see
  #" counts_not_comparable_with_2019); trip counts, yoy_change and trips per hour count it as reported (NC row).
  view: by_state is {
    group_by: state
    aggregate: agencies, trips_latest_12m, yoy_change, recovery_vs_2019, trips_per_hour_12m
    having: trips_latest_12m > 0
    order_by: trips_latest_12m desc
  }

  #" The Triangle against Sun Belt peers of similar size: how much transit each metro uses, how much service it
  #" runs, and how far each has come since 2019. Columns ending _as_reported count GoRaleigh as reported (see
  #" counts_not_comparable_with_2019), so the Triangle's ranking on trips per resident may not hold.
  # bar_chart { x=peer_metro y=trips_per_resident_12m_as_reported }
  view: peer_metros is {
    where: peer_metro is not null
    group_by: peer_metro
    aggregate:
      residents, trips_latest_12m
      trips_per_resident_12m_as_reported is trips_per_resident_12m
      trips_per_resident_2019
      recovery_vs_2019, service_vs_2019, trips_per_hour_2019
      trips_per_hour_12m_as_reported is trips_per_hour_12m
      # percent
      yoy_change_as_reported is yoy_change
    order_by: trips_per_resident_12m_as_reported desc
  }

  #" North Carolina as the Triangle, Charlotte and the rest. Trip counts and _as_reported columns count GoRaleigh as
  #" reported; recovery and service leave it out (see counts_not_comparable_with_2019).
  # bar_chart { x=nc_region y=recovery_vs_2019 }
  view: nc_regions is {
    where: state = 'NC'
    group_by: nc_region
    aggregate:
      trips_2019_as_reported is trips_2019
      trips_latest_12m_as_reported is trips_latest_12m
      recovery_vs_2019, service_vs_2019
      # percent
      yoy_change_as_reported is yoy_change
      trips_per_hour_12m_as_reported is trips_per_hour_12m
    order_by: trips_latest_12m_as_reported desc
  }

  #" Every Triangle agency: ridership, service and productivity then and now. GoRaleigh's recovery and service are
  #" blank (see counts_not_comparable_with_2019); its trip counts and trips per hour are as reported, and its latest
  #" values can't be compared with its 2019 values.
  # bar_chart { x=agency_name y=trips_latest_12m }
  view: triangle_agencies is {
    where: peer_metro = 'Triangle (Raleigh + Durham)'
    group_by: agency_name, ntd_id, urban_area
    aggregate:
      trips_2019, trips_latest_12m, recovery_vs_2019, yoy_change,
      revenue_hours_2019, revenue_hours_12m, service_vs_2019, trips_per_hour_2019, trips_per_hour_12m
    having: trips_latest_12m > 0
    order_by: trips_latest_12m desc
  }

  #" Each year's trips and service hours side by side: does ridership follow the service? 2026 is January to August
  #" only, so its row is not a full year. GoRaleigh (in NC, the Triangle and any filter that includes it)
  #" steps up from July 2024, likely a counting change (see counts_not_comparable_with_2019).
  # line_chart { x=report_year y=trips_total }
  view: service_and_trips_by_year is {
    group_by: report_year
    aggregate: trips_total, revenue_hours, trips_per_revenue_hour
    order_by: report_year
  }

  #" Fixed-route productivity for the Triangle and its peers: trips per vehicle revenue hour then and now. Recovery
  #" and service leave GoRaleigh out; trips_per_hour_12m_as_reported counts it (see counts_not_comparable_with_2019).
  # bar_chart { x=peer_metro y=trips_per_hour_12m_as_reported }
  view: peer_productivity is {
    where: peer_metro is not null and is_fixed_route
    group_by: peer_metro
    aggregate:
      recovery_vs_2019, service_vs_2019, trips_per_hour_2019
      trips_per_hour_12m_as_reported is trips_per_hour_12m
    order_by: trips_per_hour_12m_as_reported desc
  }

  #" Big systems vs small: recovery, growth, service restored and productivity by agency size band.
  # bar_chart { x=size_band y=recovery_vs_2019 }
  view: by_size_band is {
    group_by: size_band
    aggregate:
      agencies, trips_2019, trips_latest_12m, recovery_vs_2019, yoy_change,
      service_vs_2019, trips_per_hour_2019, trips_per_hour_12m
    order_by: size_band
  }

  #" Mode mix by size band: share of each band's latest-12-month trips by bus, rail and ferry.
  view: size_band_mode_mix is {
    group_by: size_band, mode_group
    aggregate:
      trips_latest_12m
      share_of_band is trips_latest_12m / all(trips_latest_12m, size_band)
    having: trips_latest_12m > 0
    order_by: size_band, trips_latest_12m desc
  }

  #" All demand-response service (ADA paratransit AND microtransit together), agencies with more than 20,000 hours in
  #" 2019, ranked by hours added since. NOT a microtransit prospect list: its top row, Access Services (LA), is 100%
  #" ADA paratransit. For microtransit prospects use ondemand_annual -> agencies_scaling_microtransit.
  view: ondemand_scaling is {
    where: is_demand_response
    group_by: state, agency is trim(agency_name), ntd_id
    aggregate: revenue_hours_2019, revenue_hours_12m, revenue_hours_change, revenue_hours_change_pct, trips_latest_12m
    having: revenue_hours_2019 > 20000
    order_by: revenue_hours_change desc
  }

  #" The 15 agencies that added the most demand-response hours (paratransit and microtransit together) since 2019,
  #" among those above 20,000 hours in 2019. For microtransit specifically use ondemand_annual.
  # bar_chart { x=agency_label y=revenue_hours_change }
  view: ondemand_added_hours is {
    where: is_demand_response
    group_by: agency_label
    aggregate: revenue_hours_change
    having: revenue_hours_2019 > 20000
    order_by: revenue_hours_change desc
    limit: 15
  }

  #" The largest agencies by 2019 trips, with recovery, service and productivity.
  view: largest_agencies is {
    group_by: agency_name, city, state
    aggregate: trips_2019, trips_latest_12m, recovery_vs_2019, yoy_change, service_vs_2019, trips_per_hour_12m
    order_by: trips_2019 desc
    limit: 10
  }

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Playbook views: named for the question a buyer asks. Each #" says who acts and what the answer means.

  #" DOES RESTORING SERVICE BRING RIDERS BACK? Agencies that restored service recovered more riders, in every size
  #" band. Agencies are split by whether they run at
  #" least their 2019 service hours (restored) or not, and compared on average recovery (latest 12 months of trips as
  #" a share of 2019). Agency-by-agency average, all modes, agencies with 2019 trips; agencies above 2x of 2019 left
  #" out as reporting outliers, and agencies flagged counts_not_comparable_with_2019 left out. WHO ACTS: a transit board
  #" or city budget office weighing service cuts or restorations; the gap between the two columns is the ridership at
  #" stake. Correlation, not proof: agencies that restored service may also have had stronger demand.
  view: does_restoring_service_bring_riders_back is {
    where: not counts_not_comparable_with_2019
    group_by: ntd_id, size_band
    aggregate: recovery_vs_2019, service_vs_2019
    having: trips_2019 > 0 and trips_latest_12m > 0 and revenue_hours_2019 > 0
  } -> {
    where: recovery_vs_2019 <= 2
    group_by: size_band
    aggregate:
      agencies_restored is count() { where: service_vs_2019 >= 1 }
      # percent
      recovery_if_restored is avg(recovery_vs_2019) { where: service_vs_2019 >= 1 }
      agencies_not_restored is count() { where: service_vs_2019 < 1 }
      # percent
      recovery_if_not_restored is avg(recovery_vs_2019) { where: service_vs_2019 < 1 }
    order_by: size_band
  }

  #" HOW IS THE TRIANGLE DOING AGAINST SIMILAR METROS? The Triangle (Raleigh + Durham urbanized areas) against Sun
  #" Belt metros of 1.0 to 1.9 million people: trips per resident, recovery, service restored, riders per service
  #" hour. Recovery leaves GoRaleigh out (see counts_not_comparable_with_2019): the rest of the Triangle is at 86% of 2019,
  #" still ahead of Charlotte at 61%; as reported, with GoRaleigh, it reads 98%. Service leaves GoRaleigh out too;
  #" columns ending _as_reported count it, so the Triangle's ranking on trips per resident may not hold. WHO ACTS: Triangle planners and the GoTriangle / Wake Transit boards (where to add service),
  #" and Charlotte, whose metro has restored only about 81% of its 2019 service and recovered the fewest riders.
  #" Population is 2020 Census, so per-resident figures overstate use in metros that have grown since.
  # bar_chart { x=peer_metro y=recovery_vs_2019 }
  view: triangle_vs_peer_metros is {
    where: peer_metro is not null
    group_by: peer_metro
    aggregate:
      recovery_vs_2019
      recovery_vs_2019_as_reported
      trips_2019_left_out
      residents, trips_latest_12m
      trips_per_resident_12m_as_reported is trips_per_resident_12m
      trips_per_resident_2019, service_vs_2019
      trips_per_hour_12m_as_reported is trips_per_hour_12m
      # percent
      yoy_change_as_reported is yoy_change
    order_by: recovery_vs_2019 desc
  }

  #" WHICH NORTH CAROLINA AGENCIES ARE STILL BELOW 2019? NC agencies with at least 100,000 trips in 2019, below
  #" their 2019 ridership in the latest 12 months, largest number of lost trips first, with service restored for
  #" comparison (an agency below 2019 on service is usually below on riders too). WHO ACTS: each agency's board and
  #" NCDOT's Integrated Mobility Division (state transit funding). GoRaleigh is not listed: its reported trips are
  #" above 2019, but they can't be compared with 2019, and its buses were at about 80% of 2019 in the year to June
  #" 2024, so it may belong on this list (see goraleigh_138_percent_check). GoDurham reads 103%.
  view: nc_agencies_below_2019 is {
    where: state = 'NC'
    group_by: agency_label, ntd_id
    aggregate:
      trips_2019, trips_latest_12m, recovery_vs_2019, service_vs_2019
      trips_lost_vs_2019 is trips_2019 - trips_latest_12m
    having: trips_2019 >= 100000 and trips_latest_12m < trips_2019
    order_by: trips_lost_vs_2019 desc
  }

  #" IS GORALEIGH REALLY AT 138% OF 2019? That figure can't be compared with 2019 (see counts_not_comparable_with_2019).
  #" Fixed-route only, one row; every figure below is a column. As reported, the latest 12 months are about 145% of
  #" 2019. In the year to June 2024, before the jump, GoRaleigh carried about 80% of its 2019 riders on about 99% of
  #" its 2019 service; service is now about 149% of 2019. On the new counting, bus riders rose about 6% in the latest
  #" 12 months on about 13% more service. Boardings per service hour: about 18 in 2019, about 14.6 in
  #" the year to June 2024 (about 12 in May and June 2024, after service hours rose 17.5% in May with no more riders),
  #" about 22.7 in July 2024, about 17.5 in the latest 12 months. How much of today's figure is real riders can't be
  #" measured from this data. July 2024 was the City's "find your fare" month before fares resumed (fare_policies),
  #" a possible lead on the cause, not a confirmed one. The whole-agency "138%" includes GoRaleigh Access. Fares resumed on 1 September
  #" 2024, after the jump (City of Raleigh; earlier announced dates were postponed). WHO ACTS: anyone quoting Triangle or GoRaleigh recovery,
  #" and GoRaleigh, which could publish a bridged series.
  view: goraleigh_138_percent_check is {
    where: ntd_id = '40007' and is_fixed_route
    aggregate:
      trips_2019, trips_latest_12m
      # percent
      reported_vs_2019 is trips_latest_12m / nullif(trips_2019, 0)
      # percent
      year_to_june_2024_vs_2019 is
        trips_total { where: report_month >= @2023-07-01 and report_month < @2024-07-01 } / nullif(trips_2019, 0)
      # percent
      service_year_to_june_2024_vs_2019 is
        revenue_hours { where: report_month >= @2023-07-01 and report_month < @2024-07-01 } / nullif(revenue_hours_2019, 0)
      # percent
      service_now_vs_2019 is revenue_hours_12m / nullif(revenue_hours_2019, 0)
      trips_per_hour_2019
      trips_per_hour_year_to_june_2024 is
        trips_per_revenue_hour { where: report_month >= @2023-07-01 and report_month < @2024-07-01 }
      trips_per_hour_june_2024 is trips_per_revenue_hour { where: report_month ? @2024-06 }
      trips_per_hour_july_2024 is trips_per_revenue_hour { where: report_month ? @2024-07 }
      trips_per_hour_12m
      # percent
      service_change_may_2024 is
        revenue_hours { where: report_month ? @2024-05 } / nullif(revenue_hours { where: report_month ? @2024-04 }, 0) - 1
      # percent
      riders_change_latest_year is yoy_change
      # percent
      service_change_latest_year is
        revenue_hours_12m / nullif(revenue_hours { where: report_month >= @2024-09-01 and report_month < @2025-09-01 }, 0) - 1
  }

  #" GoRaleigh fixed-route boardings, service hours and boardings per hour by month, March to October 2024: the July
  #" 2024 jump shows as a one-month doubling of boardings per hour.
  # line_chart { x=report_month y=trips_total }
  view: goraleigh_counting_break is {
    where: ntd_id = '40007' and is_fixed_route and report_month >= @2024-03-01 and report_month < @2024-11-01
    group_by: report_month
    aggregate: trips_total, revenue_hours, trips_per_revenue_hour
    order_by: report_month
  }
}
